var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"reference/site_type/#Site-type-API","page":"Site type","text":"See Site types and layouts for the narrative version and Site types and spin models for the physics.","title":"Site type API"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/site_type/#Abstract-type-and-interface","page":"Site type","text":"","title":"Abstract type and interface"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/site_type/#Built-in-site-types","page":"Site type","text":"","title":"Built-in site types"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/site_type/#LatticeCore.AbstractSiteType","page":"Site type","text":"AbstractSiteType\n\nAbstract supertype for site types — value-level descriptors of the physical degree of freedom living on a lattice site.\n\nConcrete subtypes are deliberately lightweight (typically singletons or thin parametric singletons) so they can be embedded in lattice structs and used as a dispatch key by MC models.\n\nRequired interface\n\nstate_type(st)::Type — the Julia type used to store a state\nrandom_state(rng, st) — sample a uniformly random state\n\nOptional interface\n\nzero_state(st) — a canonical zero state (if defined)\ndomain(st) — iterable over the state space (for small discrete site types)\nelement_type(st)::AbstractLatticeElement — which geometric element the DOF lives on. Defaults to VertexCenter. Override for bond / plaquette-centered variables such as dimer or gauge fields.\n\nSee dev/note/04_architecture/04_site_type/README.md.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LatticeCore.AbstractSiteType"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/site_type/#LatticeCore.state_type","page":"Site type","text":"state_type(st::AbstractSiteType) → Type\n\nJulia type used to store a state of site type st.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LatticeCore.state_type"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/site_type/#LatticeCore.random_state","page":"Site type","text":"random_state(rng, st::AbstractSiteType)\n\nSample a uniformly random state on site type st.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LatticeCore.random_state"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/site_type/#LatticeCore.zero_state","page":"Site type","text":"zero_state(st::AbstractSiteType)\n\nCanonical zero state (e.g. 0 for Ising, 0.0 for XY). Not all site types have to define this.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LatticeCore.zero_state"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/site_type/#LatticeCore.domain","page":"Site type","text":"domain(st::AbstractSiteType)\n\nIterable over the state space. Only meaningful for small discrete site types (Ising, Potts); continuous types leave this unimplemented.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LatticeCore.domain"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/site_type/#LatticeCore.element_type","page":"Site type","text":"element_type(st::AbstractSiteType) → AbstractLatticeElement\n\nWhich geometric element the DOF lives on. Defaults to VertexCenter. Override for bond / plaquette / cell-centered site types.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LatticeCore.element_type"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/site_type/#LatticeCore.IsingSite","page":"Site type","text":"IsingSite{T <: Integer}()\n\nIsing spin: state is ±1 stored as T (default Int8).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LatticeCore.IsingSite"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/site_type/#LatticeCore.PottsSite","page":"Site type","text":"PottsSite{Q, T <: Integer}()\n\nQ-state Potts spin: state is an integer in 1..Q stored as T.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LatticeCore.PottsSite"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/site_type/#LatticeCore.XYSite","page":"Site type","text":"XYSite{T <: AbstractFloat}()\n\nPlanar rotor: state is an angle θ ∈ [0, 2π) stored as T (default Float64).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LatticeCore.XYSite"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/site_type/#LatticeCore.HeisenbergSite","page":"Site type","text":"HeisenbergSite{T <: AbstractFloat}()\n\nClassical Heisenberg spin: state is a unit vector in ℝ³ stored as SVector{3, T} (default Float64).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LatticeCore.HeisenbergSite"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/site_type/#LatticeCore.EmptySite","page":"Site type","text":"EmptySite()\n\nVacancy / empty site: stores nothing. Useful for diluted models and as a placeholder where a site has no degrees of freedom.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LatticeCore.EmptySite"},{"category":"section","location":"concepts/#Concepts","page":"Overview","text":"This section is a set of columns: short, self-contained reads that introduce the concepts behind LatticeCore's design. They are written for someone approaching lattice simulation for the first time — or from the software-engineering side — and want to understand why the API is shaped the way it is, not just how to call it.\n\nThe goal is not to re-derive condensed-matter physics; it is to make the vocabulary LatticeCore borrows from physics legible, and to show where each concept surfaces in the code.","title":"Concepts"},{"category":"section","location":"concepts/#Where-to-read-first","page":"Overview","text":"If you are new to lattices and are here to simulate something, read in order:\n\nLattices and unit cells — what a Bravais lattice is, how unit cells and sublattices work, and why LatticeCore treats the geometric sublattice and the physical site type as independent axes.\nReciprocal lattice and Brillouin zone — the dual picture in momentum space. This is the mathematical home of reciprocal_lattice, Monkhorst–Pack meshes, and the structure factor.\nBoundary conditions — periodic vs open, cylinders, and twisted boundaries, and how each changes the physics of finite-size simulations.\nSite types and spin models — Ising, Potts, XY, Heisenberg; what the distinction means physically and how LatticeCore represents it.\nQuasiperiodic order — cut-and-project quasicrystals, Fourier modules, and why the reciprocal lattice becomes dense.","title":"Where to read first"},{"category":"section","location":"concepts/#Relation-to-the-rest-of-the-docs","page":"Overview","text":"Concept column API guide Reference\nLattices and unit cells Lattice interface AbstractLattice\nReciprocal / Brillouin Momentum space reciprocal_lattice\nBoundary conditions Boundary conditions LatticeBoundary\nSite types and spins Site types AbstractSiteType\nQuasiperiodic order Lazy / infinite HyperReciprocalLattice","title":"Relation to the rest of the docs"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/coordinates/#Coordinate-systems-and-indexing","page":"Coordinate systems","text":"This guide is a work in progress. See the coordinate API reference and the indexing reference for the exact signatures.","title":"Coordinate systems and indexing"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/coordinates/#Three-coordinate-kinds","page":"Coordinate systems","text":"LatticeCore distinguishes three ways of naming a lattice point:\n\nRealSpace{D, T} — Cartesian coordinates, stored as an SVector{D, T}.\nLatticeCoord{D} — an integer cell index plus a 1-based geometric sublattice id.\nHigherDimCoord{DPhys, DHyper, T} — a point in the host lattice of a cut-and-project quasicrystal.\n\nThey talk to each other through to_real, to_lattice, and to_hyper, all of which dispatch on the concrete lattice so that the conversion uses the lattice's basis and (if applicable) projection.","title":"Three coordinate kinds"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/coordinates/#Indexing-is-orthogonal-to-coordinates","page":"Coordinate systems","text":"Once you have a LatticeCoord, you still need a rule for turning it into a 1-based site index — that is the job of the AbstractIndexing hierarchy. LatticeCore ships RowMajor, ColMajor, and Snake with exhaustive round-trip tests.\n\nIndexing is deliberately decoupled from coordinates: a lattice can switch its indexing without changing how it talks about positions.","title":"Indexing is orthogonal to coordinates"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/coordinates/#TODO","page":"Coordinate systems","text":"Worked example: writing a custom indexing strategy (e.g. Hilbert curve) and verifying the round-trip with site_index / lattice_coord.\nWhen the sublattice-innermost convention is the wrong choice and how to override it.\nHigherDimCoord and the cut-and-project projection matrices for Penrose / Ammann–Beenker / Fibonacci.","title":"TODO"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/coordinates/#See-also","page":"Coordinate systems","text":"Concepts: Lattices and unit cells\nReference: Coordinates\nReference: Indexing","title":"See also"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/boundary/#Boundary-API","page":"Boundary","text":"See Boundary conditions guide and the concept column Boundary conditions for the narrative background.","title":"Boundary API"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/boundary/#Abstract-types","page":"Boundary","text":"","title":"Abstract types"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/boundary/#Axis-level-boundaries","page":"Boundary","text":"","title":"Axis-level boundaries"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/boundary/#Modifiers","page":"Boundary","text":"","title":"Modifiers"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/boundary/#Composite-boundary","page":"Boundary","text":"","title":"Composite boundary"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/boundary/#Hook-functions","page":"Boundary","text":"","title":"Hook functions"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/boundary/#LatticeCore.AbstractBoundaryCondition","page":"Boundary","text":"AbstractBoundaryCondition\n\nAbstract supertype for lattice boundary conditions.\n\nThe canonical concrete type is LatticeBoundary, which composes:\n\na tuple of per-axis boundary conditions (subtypes of AbstractAxisBC: PeriodicAxis, OpenAxis, TwistedAxis)\na non-topological modifier (subtype of AbstractBoundaryModifier: NoModifier, SSD)\n\nThe split is deliberate: an axis BC decides whether a candidate bond exists (and whether it carries a twist phase), while a modifier only reweights existing bonds (e.g. sine-square deformation). Multiple concerns are composed, not shoehorned into a single taxonomy.\n\nSee dev/note/04_architecture/03_boundary_and_coordinates/README.md for the design rationale.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LatticeCore.AbstractBoundaryCondition"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/boundary/#LatticeCore.AbstractAxisBC","page":"Boundary","text":"AbstractAxisBC\n\nAbstract supertype for per-axis boundary conditions. Concrete subtypes decide how a raw candidate cell index is wrapped into the lattice's index range along a single axis.\n\nSubtypes:\n\nPeriodicAxis — wraps via mod1\nOpenAxis — rejects out-of-range indices\nTwistedAxis — wraps like PeriodicAxis but attaches a phase factor to bonds that cross the boundary\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LatticeCore.AbstractAxisBC"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/boundary/#LatticeCore.AbstractBoundaryModifier","page":"Boundary","text":"AbstractBoundaryModifier\n\nAbstract supertype for boundary modifiers. A modifier does not change which bonds exist — it only reweights existing bonds through bond_weight. Examples: NoModifier, SSD.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LatticeCore.AbstractBoundaryModifier"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/boundary/#LatticeCore.PeriodicAxis","page":"Boundary","text":"Periodic axis BC: wrap-around via mod1.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LatticeCore.PeriodicAxis"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/boundary/#LatticeCore.OpenAxis","page":"Boundary","text":"Open axis BC: bonds crossing the boundary are dropped.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LatticeCore.OpenAxis"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/boundary/#LatticeCore.TwistedAxis","page":"Boundary","text":"TwistedAxis(phase::Real)\n\nPeriodic axis BC with a phase angle (radians) attached to boundary-crossing bonds. The connectivity is identical to PeriodicAxis; only the phase returned by axis_phase differs.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LatticeCore.TwistedAxis"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/boundary/#LatticeCore.NoModifier","page":"Boundary","text":"Identity modifier: every bond has weight 1.0.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LatticeCore.NoModifier"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/boundary/#LatticeCore.SSD","page":"Boundary","text":"SSD(L)\n\nSine-square deformation modifier. L is the characteristic scale used by the envelope sin²(π · x/L). The concrete weight implementation will land with the MC layer; for now this serves as a placeholder carrying the scale parameter.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LatticeCore.SSD"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/boundary/#LatticeCore.LatticeBoundary","page":"Boundary","text":"LatticeBoundary{N, A, M}(axes::NTuple{N, <:AbstractAxisBC}, modifier)\n\nComposite boundary condition for an N-dimensional lattice. Stores one AbstractAxisBC per axis plus a single AbstractBoundaryModifier. Supports mixed-axis boundary conditions natively — a cylinder is\n\nLatticeBoundary((PeriodicAxis(), OpenAxis()))\n\nThe zero-modifier form is the default.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LatticeCore.LatticeBoundary"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/boundary/#LatticeCore.apply_axis_bc","page":"Boundary","text":"apply_axis_bc(axis_bc::AbstractAxisBC, idx::Int, L::Int) → (wrapped, is_valid)\n\nApply the axis-level BC to a raw cell index idx ∈ ℤ on an axis of length L. Returns a tuple (wrapped::Int, is_valid::Bool):\n\nPeriodicAxis, TwistedAxis: (mod1(idx, L), true)\nOpenAxis: (idx, 1 <= idx <= L)\n\nThe twist phase is intentionally reported separately by axis_phase: MC code paths that do not need phases (classical Ising / XY / Heisenberg) are not forced to traffic in complex numbers.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LatticeCore.apply_axis_bc"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/boundary/#LatticeCore.axis_phase","page":"Boundary","text":"axis_phase(axis_bc::AbstractAxisBC, idx::Int, L::Int) → ComplexF64\n\nPhase factor attached to a bond that steps from a valid cell index to idx (possibly out of 1:L) along a single axis.\n\nPeriodicAxis, OpenAxis: always 1 + 0im.\nTwistedAxis(θ): cis(+θ) if idx > L, cis(-θ) if idx < 1, otherwise 1 + 0im.\n\nClassical MC paths may ignore this; quantum / flux-carrying code should multiply bond contributions by the phase.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LatticeCore.axis_phase"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/boundary/#LatticeCore.bond_weight","page":"Boundary","text":"bond_weight(modifier::AbstractBoundaryModifier, lat, i::Int, j::Int) → Float64\n\nMultiplicative weight applied to the bond (i, j) by a boundary modifier. Default implementation for NoModifier returns 1.0. SSD and other non-trivial modifiers will override this.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LatticeCore.bond_weight"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/momentum/#Momentum-space","page":"Momentum space","text":"This guide is a work in progress. See the concept column Reciprocal lattice and Brillouin zone for the mathematical background.","title":"Momentum space"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/momentum/#AbstractMomentumLattice","page":"Momentum space","text":"k-space is modelled as a subtype of AbstractMomentumLattice{D, T}, which itself inherits from AbstractLattice{D, T}. This means the num_sites, position, trait, and test-suite infrastructure written for real-space lattices also applies to k-space lattices — k-points are \"sites\" and k-vectors are \"positions\".","title":"AbstractMomentumLattice"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/momentum/#Two-concrete-subtypes","page":"Momentum space","text":"PeriodicMomentumLattice — eager k-point list built from a Bravais reciprocal basis. Constructed via monkhorst_pack or gamma_centered.\nBraggPeakSet — the quasicrystal counterpart. Same AbstractMomentumLattice interface, so the same structure_factor code paths work for both.","title":"Two concrete subtypes"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/momentum/#Entry-points","page":"Momentum space","text":"reciprocal_lattice(lat) — periodic-only.\nfourier_module(lat) — quasicrystal-only (lives in QuasiCrystal.jl).\nmomentum_lattice(lat) — trait-dispatched: picks the right one via reciprocal_support.","title":"Entry points"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/momentum/#Structure-factor","page":"Momentum space","text":"structure_factor evaluates\n\nS(mathbfk) = frac1N\n    left sum_i s_i  e^-i mathbfk cdot mathbfr_i right^2\n\nnaively in O(N) per k. FFT-based specialisations can be added without changing the API.","title":"Structure factor"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/momentum/#TODO","page":"Momentum space","text":"End-to-end example: compute S(π, π) on a 16×16 PBC square for the Néel configuration.\nHow to sample a k-path (high-symmetry lines) once that lands.\nWhen to use monkhorst_pack vs gamma_centered.","title":"TODO"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/momentum/#See-also","page":"Momentum space","text":"Concepts: Reciprocal lattice\nReference: Momentum space","title":"See also"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/lattice_element/#Lattice-element-API","page":"Lattice element","text":"Element-centering trait types that let an AbstractSiteType declare whether its DOF lives on a vertex, bond, plaquette, or cell.","title":"Lattice element API"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/lattice_element/#LatticeCore.AbstractLatticeElement","page":"Lattice element","text":"AbstractLatticeElement\n\nAbstract supertype identifying which geometric element of a lattice a given degree of freedom lives on. The default for every AbstractSiteType is VertexCenter; other element_type overrides let the interface describe bond / plaquette / cell-centered variables such as dimers, gauge links, and flux variables without breaking existing site-centered code.\n\nSee dev/note/04_architecture/04_site_type/README.md for the two-approach strategy (line-graph versus multi-layer) that uses this trait.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LatticeCore.AbstractLatticeElement"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/lattice_element/#LatticeCore.VertexCenter","page":"Lattice element","text":"Vertex-centered element: a site in the usual sense (default).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LatticeCore.VertexCenter"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/lattice_element/#LatticeCore.BondCenter","page":"Lattice element","text":"Bond-centered element: the midpoint of an edge.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LatticeCore.BondCenter"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/lattice_element/#LatticeCore.PlaquetteCenter","page":"Lattice element","text":"Plaquette-centered element: the centre of a face.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LatticeCore.PlaquetteCenter"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/lattice_element/#LatticeCore.CellCenter","page":"Lattice element","text":"Cell-centered element: the centre of a 3D (or higher) cell.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LatticeCore.CellCenter"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/site_layout/#Site-layout-API","page":"Site layout","text":"","title":"Site layout API"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/site_layout/#LatticeCore.AbstractSiteLayout","page":"Site layout","text":"AbstractSiteLayout\n\nAbstract supertype for strategies that store the per-site AbstractSiteType of a lattice. Three concrete layouts are provided, each picking a different memory / flexibility trade-off:\n\nUniformLayout — every site shares the same site type (stored once; zero per-site memory overhead)\nSublatticeLayout — each geometric sublattice has its own site type (Vector{Int} of sublattice ids)\nExplicitLayout — one AbstractSiteType per site (for disordered / quenched-random configurations)\n\nAll layouts satisfy site_type(layout, i)::AbstractSiteType.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LatticeCore.AbstractSiteLayout"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/site_layout/#LatticeCore.UniformLayout","page":"Site layout","text":"UniformLayout(st::AbstractSiteType)\n\nEvery site has the same site type. The type parameter carries the concrete site-type singleton so that downstream MC code can dispatch on it at compile time (constant folding in the hot loop).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LatticeCore.UniformLayout"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/site_layout/#LatticeCore.SublatticeLayout","page":"Site layout","text":"SublatticeLayout(by_sublattice::NTuple{N, <:AbstractSiteType}, sublattice_of::Vector{Int})\n\nPer-sublattice site type. by_sublattice is a tuple of site type instances, one per geometric sublattice; sublattice_of[i] tells which sublattice site i belongs to (1-based).\n\nThis is the natural layout for mixed-spin models (e.g. Ising on the A sublattice, XY on B).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LatticeCore.SublatticeLayout"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/site_layout/#LatticeCore.ExplicitLayout","page":"Site layout","text":"ExplicitLayout(types::Vector{<:AbstractSiteType})\n\nOne site type per site. Use for quenched disorder or any configuration where the per-site type cannot be factored through a sublattice assignment.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LatticeCore.ExplicitLayout"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/site_layout/#LatticeCore.site_type","page":"Site layout","text":"site_type(layout::AbstractSiteLayout, i::Int) → AbstractSiteType\n\nReturn the site type stored at site index i.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LatticeCore.site_type"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/lazy_infinite/#Lazy-/-infinite-lattices-API","page":"Lazy / infinite","text":"See Lazy / infinite lattices guide for the narrative version.","title":"Lazy / infinite lattices API"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/lazy_infinite/#LatticeCore.materialize","page":"Lazy / infinite","text":"materialize(abstract; kwargs...) → AbstractLattice\n\nMaterialise an infinite / conceptually-infinite abstract lattice into a finite AbstractLattice up to the given cutoff.\n\nThis is the entry point for working with infinite structures (quasicrystals beyond a cutoff radius, Fibonacci / L-system substitutions beyond a certain depth, etc.) inside LatticeCore. It is intentionally generic with no typed supertype: any package can ship its own \"infinite abstract\" type and implement materialize without having to inherit from a LatticeCore hierarchy.\n\nCutoff convention\n\nThe meaning of the cutoff keyword argument(s) is implementation- defined. Typical choices are:\n\ndepth::Int — substitution depth (Fibonacci, L-system)\nradius::Real — spatial radius (Penrose cut-and-project)\ndims::NTuple{D, Int} — explicit per-axis sizes\n\nThe returned lattice must be is_finite == true, i.e. its size_trait should be a FiniteSize, so it can be fed directly into Monte Carlo algorithms guarded by require_finite.\n\nSee dev/note/04_architecture/06_lazy_infinite/README.md for the infinite-abstract ↔ finite-materialisation design pattern, and for the parallel pattern in 05 Part B (HyperReciprocalLattice → BraggPeakSet).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LatticeCore.materialize"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/lazy_infinite/#LatticeCore.require_finite","page":"Lazy / infinite","text":"require_finite(lat::AbstractLattice)\n\nAssert that lat is a finite lattice. Throws ArgumentError if is_finite returns false.\n\nIntended as a guard at the entry point of Monte Carlo algorithms or any routine that cannot operate on an infinite or not-yet-materialised lattice. Returns nothing on success.\n\nExample\n\nfunction run!(rng, state, lat::AbstractLattice, model, alg; kwargs...)\n    require_finite(lat)\n    # ... safe to walk the full site list from here ...\nend\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LatticeCore.require_finite"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/indexing/#Indexing-API","page":"Indexing","text":"See Coordinate systems and indexing for the narrative version.","title":"Indexing API"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/indexing/#Abstract-type","page":"Indexing","text":"","title":"Abstract type"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/indexing/#Concrete-strategies","page":"Indexing","text":"","title":"Concrete strategies"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/indexing/#Generic-functions","page":"Indexing","text":"","title":"Generic functions"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/indexing/#LatticeCore.AbstractIndexing","page":"Indexing","text":"AbstractIndexing\n\nAbstract supertype for strategies that linearise a LatticeCoord into a 1-based site index. Concrete subtypes determine the ordering of sites along axes and the placement of sublattices within each cell.\n\nThe indexing method is deliberately decoupled from the coordinate system: a lattice may switch its AbstractIndexing without changing how it describes positions. See dev/note/04_architecture/03_boundary_and_coordinates/README.md for the design rationale.\n\nRequired interface (per concrete indexing / dimension)\n\nsite_index(indexing, dims::NTuple{D, Int}, nsub::Int, coord::LatticeCoord{D})::Int\nlattice_coord(indexing, dims::NTuple{D, Int}, nsub::Int, i::Int)::LatticeCoord{D}\n\nThese two methods must round-trip: site_index ∘ lattice_coord is the identity on 1:prod(dims) * nsub.\n\nSublattice convention\n\nThe sublattice is the innermost (fastest) index: sites within a single cell are contiguous. For nsub == 1 this reduces to the usual single-sublattice formulas and can be read at a glance.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LatticeCore.AbstractIndexing"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/indexing/#LatticeCore.RowMajor","page":"Indexing","text":"Row-major (C-style): x is the fastest axis within a row.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LatticeCore.RowMajor"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/indexing/#LatticeCore.ColMajor","page":"Indexing","text":"Column-major (Fortran-style): y is the fastest axis.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LatticeCore.ColMajor"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/indexing/#LatticeCore.Snake","page":"Indexing","text":"Snake\n\nBoustrophedon (snake) indexing: row-major layout with alternating row direction. Useful when physical adjacency between neighbouring indices matters (e.g. for certain serialisation layouts).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LatticeCore.Snake"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/indexing/#LatticeCore.site_index","page":"Indexing","text":"site_index(indexing, dims, nsub, coord) → Int\n\nLinearise coord into a 1-based site index. See AbstractIndexing for the required contract.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LatticeCore.site_index"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/indexing/#LatticeCore.lattice_coord","page":"Indexing","text":"lattice_coord(indexing, dims, nsub, i) → LatticeCoord\n\nInverse of site_index.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LatticeCore.lattice_coord"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/traits/#Traits","page":"Traits","text":"LatticeCore uses small value types (Holy traits) to express optional capabilities and invariants without inflating the concrete lattice's type parameters. See Lattice interface for where each trait is consumed.","title":"Traits"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/traits/#Topology","page":"Traits","text":"","title":"Topology"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/traits/#Periodicity","page":"Traits","text":"","title":"Periodicity"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/traits/#Reciprocal-space-support","page":"Traits","text":"","title":"Reciprocal-space support"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/traits/#Size","page":"Traits","text":"","title":"Size"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/traits/#LatticeCore.TopologyTrait","page":"Traits","text":"TopologyTrait{Name}\n\nSingleton trait carrying the topology name as a type parameter (e.g. TopologyTrait{:Square}, TopologyTrait{:Honeycomb}, TopologyTrait{:Penrose}). Used for dispatch in topology-specific helpers such as high-symmetry-point tables.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LatticeCore.TopologyTrait"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/traits/#LatticeCore.topology","page":"Traits","text":"Default topology trait: TopologyTrait{:unknown}().\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LatticeCore.topology"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/traits/#LatticeCore.Periodic","page":"Traits","text":"Periodicity trait marker for Bravais-lattice-like structures.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LatticeCore.Periodic"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/traits/#LatticeCore.Aperiodic","page":"Traits","text":"Periodicity trait marker for aperiodic structures (e.g. quasicrystals).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LatticeCore.Aperiodic"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/traits/#LatticeCore.periodicity","page":"Traits","text":"Default periodicity: Aperiodic().\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LatticeCore.periodicity"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/traits/#LatticeCore.is_bipartite","page":"Traits","text":"Default bipartite flag: false.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LatticeCore.is_bipartite"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/traits/#LatticeCore.AbstractReciprocalSupport","page":"Traits","text":"AbstractReciprocalSupport\n\nTrait indicating what kind of k-space representation a lattice admits.\n\nSubtypes:\n\nHasReciprocal: standard Bravais reciprocal lattice.\nHasFourierModule: dense Fourier module arising from a cut-and-project quasicrystal.\nNoReciprocal: neither of the above (e.g. generic graph).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LatticeCore.AbstractReciprocalSupport"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/traits/#LatticeCore.HasReciprocal","page":"Traits","text":"Trait: the lattice has a standard Bravais reciprocal lattice.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LatticeCore.HasReciprocal"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/traits/#LatticeCore.HasFourierModule","page":"Traits","text":"Trait: the lattice has a Fourier module (quasicrystal).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LatticeCore.HasFourierModule"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/traits/#LatticeCore.NoReciprocal","page":"Traits","text":"Trait: the lattice has no k-space representation.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LatticeCore.NoReciprocal"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/traits/#LatticeCore.reciprocal_support","page":"Traits","text":"Default reciprocal support: NoReciprocal().\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LatticeCore.reciprocal_support"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/traits/#LatticeCore.AbstractSizeTrait","page":"Traits","text":"AbstractSizeTrait\n\nTrait describing whether a lattice has a finite, infinite, or finitely-materializable-but-conceptually-infinite extent.\n\nSubtypes:\n\nFiniteSize: ordinary finite lattice.\nInfiniteSize: true infinite lattice (used for analytic/spectral work; MC is not applicable).\nQuasiInfiniteSize: conceptually infinite but materialized up to a cutoff (e.g. Penrose radius, Fibonacci depth).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LatticeCore.AbstractSizeTrait"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/traits/#LatticeCore.FiniteSize","page":"Traits","text":"FiniteSize{D}(dims::NTuple{D, Int})\n\nSize trait for an ordinary finite lattice with the given per-axis cell counts.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LatticeCore.FiniteSize"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/traits/#LatticeCore.InfiniteSize","page":"Traits","text":"InfiniteSize()\n\nSize trait for a true infinite lattice. MC cannot run on such a lattice; spectral / analytic calculations only.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LatticeCore.InfiniteSize"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/traits/#LatticeCore.QuasiInfiniteSize","page":"Traits","text":"QuasiInfiniteSize{T}(cutoff::T)\n\nSize trait for a conceptually infinite lattice that has been (or will be) materialized up to a finite cutoff. The cutoff may represent a radius, a substitution depth, or any other scale parameter native to the lattice family.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LatticeCore.QuasiInfiniteSize"},{"category":"section","location":"concepts/lattice_and_unit_cells/#Lattices-and-unit-cells","page":"Lattices and unit cells","text":"A lattice is the skeleton physicists use to describe a crystal: an infinite, regular pattern of points in space obtained by repeatedly translating a finite motif. Everything LatticeCore does — storing positions, walking neighbours, computing structure factors — is organised around this picture, so it is worth spending a few minutes making the vocabulary precise.","title":"Lattices and unit cells"},{"category":"section","location":"concepts/lattice_and_unit_cells/#Bravais-lattices","page":"Lattices and unit cells","text":"A Bravais lattice in d dimensions is the set of points\n\nmathbfR = n_1 mathbfa_1 + n_2 mathbfa_2 + dots + n_d mathbfa_d\nqquad n_i in mathbbZ\n\nwhere the primitive vectors mathbfa_1 dots mathbfa_d are linearly independent and fixed. The matrix whose columns are the mathbfa_i is the real-space basis A. In LatticeCore a concrete lattice exposes it via basis_vectors(lat).\n\nTwo Bravais lattices are the same if and only if they share a basis up to an integer (unimodular) linear transformation. That means the basis is never unique — the honest square lattice\n\nmathbfa_1 = (1 0) quad mathbfa_2 = (0 1)\n\nis the same Bravais lattice as the skewed choice\n\nmathbfa_1 = (1 0) quad mathbfa_2 = (1 1)\n\nand the code treats both as equivalent inputs. What is canonical is the set of lattice points mathbfR and everything that can be computed from it: neighbour distances, reciprocal basis (see Reciprocal lattice and Brillouin zone), density.\n\nTwo-dimensional Bravais lattices come in exactly five types (oblique, rectangular, centred rectangular, hexagonal, square), three-dimensional ones in fourteen. LatticeCore's reference implementations (SimpleSquareLattice, LineLattice) are the square and 1D cases with unit spacing, which is enough to exercise every piece of the interface.","title":"Bravais lattices"},{"category":"section","location":"concepts/lattice_and_unit_cells/#Unit-cells-and-sublattices","page":"Lattices and unit cells","text":"A crystal is usually not just a Bravais lattice. Each lattice point carries a basis — a finite cluster of atoms, or (for us) a finite cluster of sites. Copies of that cluster sit at every lattice point, and together they make the full crystal:\n\nmathrmCrystal = mathrmBravais lattice + mathrmmotif\n\nThe cluster is called the unit cell, and each member of the cluster is a sublattice. A honeycomb lattice is a triangular Bravais lattice with a two-site motif — one A site, one B site — and that is exactly why the A and B sublattices never coincide.\n\nLatticeCore records sublattice membership inside the LatticeCoord type:\n\nstruct LatticeCoord{D}\n    cell::NTuple{D, Int}   # which Bravais cell\n    sublattice::Int        # which site inside the cell\nend\n\nA coordinate like LatticeCoord((3, 2), 1) means \"the A site of the (3, 2) cell\". This lets the same cell index talk to multiple sublattices without any tuple juggling.","title":"Unit cells and sublattices"},{"category":"section","location":"concepts/lattice_and_unit_cells/#Sublattice-vs-site-type","page":"Lattices and unit cells","text":"There are two useful ways to label a site, and they are genuinely different:\n\nGeometric sublattice — where in the unit cell the site sits (A, B, C, ...). This is pure geometry and it is what LatticeCoord.sublattice stores.\nSite type — what physical degree of freedom lives on the site (Ising spin, XY rotor, Heisenberg vector, vacancy, gauge link, ...). This is captured by AbstractSiteType and AbstractSiteLayout.\n\nConflating the two is the single most common modelling bug in a mixed-spin simulation. LatticeCore keeps them on separate axes deliberately: a honeycomb lattice can have an Ising site on the A sublattice and an XY site on the B sublattice, and the code never has to decide which field to ask. See Site types and spin models for the site type side.","title":"Sublattice vs site type"},{"category":"section","location":"concepts/lattice_and_unit_cells/#Periodicity,-bipartiteness,-and-coordination-number","page":"Lattices and unit cells","text":"Three numbers describe a Bravais-like lattice at a glance:\n\nThe coordination number z — how many nearest neighbours a site has. It is a topology invariant (square lattice: 4, triangular: 6, honeycomb: 3, kagome: 4), visible in LatticeCore through length(neighbors(lat, i)) for an interior site.\nThe bipartiteness of the neighbour graph. A lattice is bipartite if the sites can be two-coloured so that every bond connects different colours. Square and honeycomb are bipartite; triangular is not. Bipartiteness controls whether a Neel antiferromagnetic ground state even exists and is exposed as is_bipartite.\nThe periodicity of the underlying crystal. A Bravais crystal is periodic by construction; a Penrose tiling is aperiodic even though it is highly ordered. LatticeCore captures this with the Periodic / Aperiodic trait attached to every lattice.","title":"Periodicity, bipartiteness, and coordination number"},{"category":"section","location":"concepts/lattice_and_unit_cells/#Why-this-matters-for-the-API","page":"Lattices and unit cells","text":"Once these concepts are named, the LatticeCore interface almost writes itself:\n\nposition(lat, i) returns a real-space point — a sum of primitive vectors plus a sublattice offset. It is the one function that carries the full geometric information of the lattice.\nneighbors(lat, i) encodes the topology — which other sites are connected by a bond, independent of positions.\nboundary(lat) decides what happens at the edges of a finite piece of the otherwise infinite Bravais lattice. That is the subject of Boundary conditions.\n\nThe rest of the package (site types, reciprocal lattice, structure factor, lazy materialisation) is built out of these three calls.","title":"Why this matters for the API"},{"category":"section","location":"concepts/lattice_and_unit_cells/#Further-reading","page":"Lattices and unit cells","text":"N. W. Ashcroft and N. D. Mermin, Solid State Physics, Ch. 4–5 (Bravais lattices and reciprocal lattices).\nC. Kittel, Introduction to Solid State Physics, Ch. 1–2.\nS. Sachdev, Quantum Phase Transitions, Ch. 1 (for the role of lattice geometry in ordering transitions).","title":"Further reading"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/site_type/#Site-types-and-layouts","page":"Site types and layouts","text":"This guide is a work in progress. See the concept column Site types and spin models for the physics.","title":"Site types and layouts"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/site_type/#Site-type-sublattice","page":"Site types and layouts","text":"LatticeCore separates two ideas that are traditionally conflated:\n\nGeometric sublattice — where inside a unit cell the site sits (A, B, C, ...). Stored in LatticeCoord.sublattice.\nSite type — what physical degree of freedom lives on the site (Ising spin, XY rotor, Heisenberg vector, ...). Described by AbstractSiteType and stored via an AbstractSiteLayout.\n\nThese axes are orthogonal: the same geometric sublattice can host any site type, and the same site type can sit on any sublattice.","title":"Site type ≠ sublattice"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/site_type/#Three-layouts","page":"Site types and layouts","text":"Layout When to use\nUniformLayout Every site has the same type (most models)\nSublatticeLayout Mixed-spin: A = Ising, B = XY, ...\nExplicitLayout Per-site heterogeneity (quenched disorder)\n\nUniformLayout has zero per-site memory overhead and lets the MC hot loop constant-fold the site type.","title":"Three layouts"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/site_type/#Built-in-site-types","page":"Site types and layouts","text":"IsingSite, PottsSite{Q}, XYSite, HeisenbergSite, EmptySite — see the site type reference for state_type / random_state / domain of each.","title":"Built-in site types"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/site_type/#Element-centering","page":"Site types and layouts","text":"The element_type trait lets a site type declare that its DOF lives on a BondCenter, PlaquetteCenter, or CellCenter rather than the default VertexCenter. This reserves headroom for dimer variables, gauge links, and flux fields without a breaking rewrite.","title":"Element centering"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/site_type/#TODO","page":"Site types and layouts","text":"Mixed-spin example end-to-end: build a honeycomb with Ising A / XY B, then compute site_type(lat, i) for each i.\nCustom site type tutorial: add a bond-centered dimer variable.\nIntegration with Monte Carlo models in Lattice2DMonteCarlo.jl.","title":"TODO"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/site_type/#See-also","page":"Site types and layouts","text":"Concepts: Site types and spin models\nReference: Site type\nReference: Site layout\nReference: Lattice element","title":"See also"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/momentum/#Momentum-space-API","page":"Momentum space","text":"See Momentum space guide for the narrative version and Reciprocal lattice and Brillouin zone for the mathematics.","title":"Momentum space API"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/momentum/#Abstract-types","page":"Momentum space","text":"","title":"Abstract types"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/momentum/#Concrete-momentum-lattices","page":"Momentum space","text":"","title":"Concrete momentum lattices"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/momentum/#Interface","page":"Momentum space","text":"","title":"Interface"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/momentum/#Mesh-constructors","page":"Momentum space","text":"","title":"Mesh constructors"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/momentum/#Entry-points","page":"Momentum space","text":"","title":"Entry points"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/momentum/#Structure-factor","page":"Momentum space","text":"","title":"Structure factor"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/momentum/#Quasicrystal-Fourier-module-skeletons","page":"Momentum space","text":"","title":"Quasicrystal Fourier-module skeletons"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/momentum/#LatticeCore.AbstractMomentumLattice","page":"Momentum space","text":"AbstractMomentumLattice{D, T} <: AbstractLattice{D, T}\n\nAbstract supertype for k-space lattices. A momentum lattice is itself an AbstractLattice — its \"sites\" are k-points, its \"positions\" are k-vectors in the reciprocal basis — so 02's lattice interface (num_sites, position, traits, test suite) can be re-used for k-space work. See dev/note/04_architecture/05_momentum_space.\n\nRequired interface for concrete subtypes\n\nnum_k_points(ml)::Int\nk_point(ml, i::Int)::SVector{D, T}\nreciprocal_basis(ml)::SMatrix{D, D, T}\n\nThe graph-neighbour side of AbstractLattice (neighbors, bonds) is deliberately vacuous for momentum lattices: k-space is not a graph in the same sense. Concrete momentum lattices return an empty neighbour list; MC code should never walk them as a graph.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LatticeCore.AbstractMomentumLattice"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/momentum/#LatticeCore.PeriodicMomentumLattice","page":"Momentum space","text":"PeriodicMomentumLattice{D, T}\n\nConcrete momentum lattice for a Bravais periodic lattice. Stores the reciprocal basis, the mesh dimensions, the Γ-offset, and an explicit list of k-points (eager construction).\n\nConstruct via monkhorst_pack or gamma_centered rather than calling the struct directly.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LatticeCore.PeriodicMomentumLattice"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/momentum/#LatticeCore.num_k_points","page":"Momentum space","text":"num_k_points(ml::AbstractMomentumLattice) → Int\n\nNumber of k-points stored in the lattice.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LatticeCore.num_k_points"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/momentum/#LatticeCore.k_point","page":"Momentum space","text":"k_point(ml::AbstractMomentumLattice, i::Int) → SVector{D, T}\n\nThe i-th k-vector in real (Cartesian) units — i.e. already multiplied through the reciprocal basis.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LatticeCore.k_point"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/momentum/#LatticeCore.reciprocal_basis","page":"Momentum space","text":"reciprocal_basis(ml::AbstractMomentumLattice) → SMatrix{D, D, T}\n\nReciprocal-space basis matrix. Columns are the primitive reciprocal lattice vectors.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LatticeCore.reciprocal_basis"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/momentum/#LatticeCore.monkhorst_pack","page":"Momentum space","text":"monkhorst_pack(basis::SMatrix{D, D, T}, mesh::NTuple{D, Int})\n    → PeriodicMomentumLattice{D, T}\n\nConstruct a Monkhorst–Pack half-shifted mesh over the Brillouin zone spanned by basis. For each axis the fractional k-coordinates are\n\nfrac_i ∈ { (n + 0.5) / N - 0.5 | n = 0, …, N - 1 }\n\nso the mesh is centred at Γ and avoids the BZ edges.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LatticeCore.monkhorst_pack"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/momentum/#LatticeCore.gamma_centered","page":"Momentum space","text":"gamma_centered(basis::SMatrix{D, D, T}, mesh::NTuple{D, Int})\n    → PeriodicMomentumLattice{D, T}\n\nConstruct a Γ-centred regular mesh. Fractional k-coordinates are n / N with n = 0, …, N - 1, so the mesh includes Γ and walks to but not through the BZ edge.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LatticeCore.gamma_centered"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/momentum/#LatticeCore.reciprocal_lattice","page":"Momentum space","text":"reciprocal_lattice(lat::AbstractLattice) → PeriodicMomentumLattice\n\nConstruct the reciprocal lattice for a Bravais-like lat. Concrete lattices are expected to specialise this method; the fallback throws.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LatticeCore.reciprocal_lattice"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/momentum/#LatticeCore.fourier_module","page":"Momentum space","text":"fourier_module(lat::AbstractLattice) → AbstractMomentumLattice\n\nQuasicrystal-side entry point: construct the discrete Fourier module (Bragg peak set) for a cut-and-project lattice. Concrete quasicrystal lattices implement this in their own package; the fallback throws.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LatticeCore.fourier_module"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/momentum/#LatticeCore.momentum_lattice","page":"Momentum space","text":"momentum_lattice(lat::AbstractLattice) → AbstractMomentumLattice\n\nUnified trait-dispatched entry point. Returns the reciprocal lattice for Bravais-like structures, the Fourier module for quasicrystals, and throws for lattices without any k-space representation.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LatticeCore.momentum_lattice"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/momentum/#LatticeCore.structure_factor","page":"Momentum space","text":"structure_factor(lat, state, k::SVector) → Float64\n\nScalar structure factor at a single k-point:\n\nS(k) = (1/N) |⟨ Σ_i s_i exp(-i k · r_i) ⟩|²\n\ncomputed on a single snapshot (no thermal averaging). The default implementation is naive O(N) per k-point; FFT-based specialisations on regular meshes can be added later without changing the API.\n\n\n\n\n\nstructure_factor(lat, state, ml::AbstractMomentumLattice) → Vector{Float64}\n\nEvaluate the structure factor at every k-point of ml. Naive O(N · M) where M = num_k_points(ml).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LatticeCore.structure_factor"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/momentum/#LatticeCore.AcceptanceWindow","page":"Momentum space","text":"AcceptanceWindow\n\nAbstract supertype for acceptance windows in the internal (perp) space of a cut-and-project quasicrystal. Concrete windows (PolygonalWindow, IntervalWindow, etc.) live in QuasiCrystal.jl.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LatticeCore.AcceptanceWindow"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/momentum/#LatticeCore.HyperReciprocalLattice","page":"Momentum space","text":"HyperReciprocalLattice{DPhys, DHyper, T}\n\nInfinite-abstract representation of a quasicrystal's reciprocal structure: the higher-dimensional reciprocal basis, the parallel and perpendicular projections, and the acceptance window used to decide peak intensities.\n\nConcrete construction (building the projections, sampling peaks) lives in QuasiCrystal.jl.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LatticeCore.HyperReciprocalLattice"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/momentum/#LatticeCore.BraggPeakSet","page":"Momentum space","text":"BraggPeakSet{DPhys, T}\n\nFinite materialisation of a HyperReciprocalLattice up to a cutoff radius: a list of physical-space k-positions with intensities and a back-pointer to the higher-dimensional indices that generated them.\n\nBeing a subtype of AbstractMomentumLattice, a BraggPeakSet can be passed straight to structure_factor or to a StructureFactorObserver so the same code paths work for periodic and quasiperiodic lattices.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LatticeCore.BraggPeakSet"},{"category":"section","location":"design/#Design-notes","page":"Design notes","text":"This page summarises the architectural decisions that shape LatticeCore. The long-form notes — chapter-by-chapter rationale, alternatives considered, cross-references between chapters — live outside the package in the parent workspace's dev/note/04_architecture/ tree.","title":"Design notes"},{"category":"section","location":"design/#Five-orthogonal-axes","page":"Design notes","text":"LatticeCore models every lattice along five independent axes. The cross-references point to the chapter of dev/note/04_architecture/ where each axis is designed.\n\nAxis Lives in Chapter\nTopology / graph Traits.jl + concrete lattice's neighbors 02\nBoundary condition LatticeBoundary, AbstractAxisBC 03\nCoordinate system AbstractCoordinate + AbstractIndexing 03\nGeometric sublattice LatticeCoord.sublattice + sublattice(lat, i) 04\nPhysical site type AbstractSiteType + AbstractSiteLayout 04\n\nMomentum space (chapter 05) and the size trait hierarchy / materialisation pattern (chapter 06) sit on top of these five axes and reuse the same interface machinery.","title":"Five orthogonal axes"},{"category":"section","location":"design/#Key-rules","page":"Design notes","text":"Two type parameters only. AbstractLattice{D, T}. The rest is field storage and dispatch, so the type system stays small.\nNo isa branching in hot paths. Every variant point goes through multiple dispatch or a Holy trait. This keeps extensions additive: a new axis BC is a new subtype plus a new method, not an edit to an if chain.\nSublattice and site type are different concepts. The geometric sublattice is where a site sits inside the unit cell; the site type is what lives on it. See Lattices and unit cells.\nPer-axis boundary conditions. Cylinders (PBC × OBC) and twisted periodic lattices drop out of the same type system as uniform PBC.\nInfinite structures are expressed abstractly and materialised at a cutoff. The same pattern applies in real space (materialize) and in reciprocal space (BraggPeakSet as a finite snapshot of HyperReciprocalLattice).\nLazy-friendly interface. position(lat, i) and neighbors(lat, i) are pinpoint; positions(lat) and bonds(lat) are iterators. No method requires enumerating every site up front.\nBreaking changes are cheap in 0.x. The whole stack is pre-1.0; the VersionCheck workflow enforces a version bump on every PR, and tags track every merged change.","title":"Key rules"},{"category":"section","location":"design/#Where-the-notes-live","page":"Design notes","text":"The authoritative design chapters are under dev/note/04_architecture/ in the parent workspace:\n\n01_package_layout/ — why LatticeCore is a separate repo\n02_lattice_interface/ — the AbstractLattice contract\n03_boundary_and_coordinates/ — per-axis BC and coordinate system\n04_site_type/ — site types, layouts, and element centering\n05_momentum_space/ — reciprocal lattices, Bragg peaks, structure factor\n06_lazy_infinite/ — size traits and materialisation\n07_mc_layer/ — Monte Carlo consumer layer (lives in Lattice2DMonteCarlo.jl)\n\nThey are checked into the parent workspace rather than into LatticeCore itself so that cross-package architectural changes can be discussed in one place.","title":"Where the notes live"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/lattice/#AbstractLattice-API","page":"Lattice","text":"Auto-generated reference for the AbstractLattice interface. See Lattice interface for the narrative version and Lattices and unit cells for the physical background.","title":"AbstractLattice API"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/lattice/#Abstract-type","page":"Lattice","text":"","title":"Abstract type"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/lattice/#Required-interface","page":"Lattice","text":"","title":"Required interface"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/lattice/#Derived-/-default-helpers","page":"Lattice","text":"","title":"Derived / default helpers"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/lattice/#LatticeCore.AbstractLattice","page":"Lattice","text":"AbstractLattice{D, T}\n\nAbstract supertype for lattice types. Type parameters:\n\nD: spatial dimension (integer literal)\nT: numeric type for real-space positions (typically Float64)\n\nRequired interface (concrete subtypes must implement)\n\nposition(lat, i)::SVector{D, T}\nneighbors(lat, i) — iterable of neighbor site indices\nboundary(lat) — returns an AbstractBoundaryCondition\nsize_trait(lat)::AbstractSizeTrait\nnum_sites(lat)::Int — required for FiniteSize / QuasiInfiniteSize lattices; InfiniteSize lattices should throw DomainError.\n\nOptional interface (defaults provided)\n\npositions(lat) — defaults to an iterator built from position\nbonds(lat) — defaults to an iterator built from neighbors\nneighbor_bonds(lat, i) — defaults to an iterator built from neighbors\ntopology(lat) — TopologyTrait{:unknown}()\nperiodicity(lat) — Aperiodic()\nis_bipartite(lat) — false\nreciprocal_support(lat) — NoReciprocal()\nis_finite(lat) — derived from size_trait\n\nSee dev/note/04_architecture/02_lattice_interface/README.md for the design rationale.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LatticeCore.AbstractLattice"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/lattice/#LatticeCore.num_sites","page":"Lattice","text":"num_sites(lat::AbstractLattice)::Int\n\nNumber of sites in the lattice. Must be implemented by finite concrete types. InfiniteSize lattices should throw DomainError.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LatticeCore.num_sites"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/lattice/#Base.position","page":"Lattice","text":"position(lat::AbstractLattice{D, T}, i::Int)::SVector{D, T}\n\nReal-space position of site i. This extends Base.position so that downstream code can write position(lat, i) directly after using LatticeCore.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.position"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/lattice/#LatticeCore.neighbors","page":"Lattice","text":"neighbors(lat::AbstractLattice, i::Int)\n\nIndices of sites adjacent to site i (nearest neighbors by default). Concrete types may additionally define neighbors(lat, i; shell) or neighbors(lat, i, shell::Int) to support higher shells.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LatticeCore.neighbors"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/lattice/#LatticeCore.boundary","page":"Lattice","text":"boundary(lat::AbstractLattice)\n\nThe lattice's boundary condition (subtype of AbstractBoundaryCondition, defined in BoundaryCondition.jl).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LatticeCore.boundary"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/lattice/#LatticeCore.size_trait","page":"Lattice","text":"size_trait(lat::AbstractLattice) → AbstractSizeTrait\n\nSize trait describing the lattice's extent. Must be implemented by concrete types.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LatticeCore.size_trait"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/lattice/#LatticeCore.site_layout","page":"Lattice","text":"site_layout(lat::AbstractLattice) → AbstractSiteLayout\n\nReturn the lattice's site layout. Concrete lattices must either implement this method directly or hold an AbstractSiteLayout in a field of that name.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LatticeCore.site_layout"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/lattice/#LatticeCore.dimension","page":"Lattice","text":"dimension(lat) → Int\n\nSpatial dimension D of the lattice.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LatticeCore.dimension"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/lattice/#LatticeCore.positions","page":"Lattice","text":"positions(lat::AbstractLattice)\n\nIterator over all positions. Default implementation lazily constructs from num_sites and position; works only for finite lattices. Concrete types may override for efficiency.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LatticeCore.positions"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/lattice/#LatticeCore.is_finite","page":"Lattice","text":"is_finite(lat::AbstractLattice) → Bool\n\ntrue if size_trait(lat) is a FiniteSize. Monte Carlo algorithms should guard against non-finite lattices with this predicate.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LatticeCore.is_finite"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/lattice/#LatticeCore.num_sublattices","page":"Lattice","text":"num_sublattices(lat::AbstractLattice) → Int\n\nNumber of geometric sublattices in the lattice. Defaults to 1 so lattices that are not sublattice-aware need not override.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LatticeCore.num_sublattices"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/lattice/#LatticeCore.sublattice","page":"Lattice","text":"sublattice(lat::AbstractLattice, i::Int) → Int\n\nGeometric sublattice id (1-based) of site i. Defaults to 1, matching the default num_sublattices of 1.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LatticeCore.sublattice"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/boundary/#Boundary-conditions","page":"Boundary conditions","text":"This guide is a work in progress. For now, see the concept column Boundary conditions for the physics, and the boundary API reference for the exact signatures.","title":"Boundary conditions"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/boundary/#The-picture","page":"Boundary conditions","text":"A boundary condition in LatticeCore is a composite object:\n\nLatticeBoundary(\n    axes     = (PeriodicAxis(), OpenAxis()),   # one AbstractAxisBC per axis\n    modifier = NoModifier(),                   # non-topological reweighting\n)\n\nThe axes decide which candidate bonds exist — they wrap, clamp, or attach a phase to a bond that would otherwise leave the sample. The modifier decides how existing bonds are weighted (e.g. sine-square deformation). Splitting the two concerns this way means a cylinder and an SSD modification can be composed without any core code change.","title":"The picture"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/boundary/#What-LatticeCore-ships-today","page":"Boundary conditions","text":"Axis BCs: PeriodicAxis, OpenAxis, TwistedAxis.\nModifiers: NoModifier, SSD (type only; the weighting rule will land with the MC layer).\nHooks: apply_axis_bc, axis_phase, bond_weight.","title":"What LatticeCore ships today"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/boundary/#TODO","page":"Boundary conditions","text":"Worked cylinder example on a 3×4 square lattice, with a picture of the neighbour graph.\nHow reference lattices use apply_axis_bc inside neighbors.\nAdding a new axis BC subtype from scratch.","title":"TODO"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/boundary/#See-also","page":"Boundary conditions","text":"Concepts: Boundary conditions\nReference: Boundary","title":"See also"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/bond/#Bond-API","page":"Bond","text":"","title":"Bond API"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/bond/#LatticeCore.Bond","page":"Bond","text":"Bond{D, T}\n\nA bond (edge) connecting two sites of a lattice.\n\nFields\n\ni::Int — source site index\nj::Int — target site index\nvector::SVector{D, T} — displacement position(j) - position(i), wrapped by the lattice's boundary condition if applicable\ntype::Symbol — bond type tag (e.g. :nearest, :next_nearest, :dimer_strong). Used as a dispatch key by anisotropic models (see the 07 MC layer design note).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LatticeCore.Bond"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/bond/#LatticeCore.bonds","page":"Bond","text":"bonds(lat::AbstractLattice)\n\nIterator of all bonds in the lattice. The default implementation builds Bond objects on the fly from neighbors(lat, i) using the :nearest tag. Concrete lattices may override this for efficiency or to attach non-default bond types (e.g. dimer-strong vs dimer-weak).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LatticeCore.bonds"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/bond/#LatticeCore.neighbor_bonds","page":"Bond","text":"neighbor_bonds(lat::AbstractLattice, i::Int)\n\nIterator of bonds incident to site i. The default implementation builds Bond objects from neighbors(lat, i) using the :nearest tag. This is the canonical entry point the 07 MC layer uses to walk interactions involving a given site.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LatticeCore.neighbor_bonds"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/bond/#LatticeCore.bond_center","page":"Bond","text":"bond_center(lat::AbstractLattice, bond::Bond) → SVector{D, T}\n\nGeometric center (midpoint) of the bond in real space. Useful for bond-centered observables and for position-dependent bond modifiers such as sine-square deformation.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LatticeCore.bond_center"},{"category":"section","location":"getting_started/#Getting-Started","page":"Getting Started","text":"","title":"Getting Started"},{"category":"section","location":"getting_started/#Installation","page":"Getting Started","text":"LatticeCore is not yet registered in the Julia General registry. Install it directly from GitHub:\n\nusing Pkg\nPkg.add(url = \"https://github.com/sotashimozono/LatticeCore.jl.git\")","title":"Installation"},{"category":"section","location":"getting_started/#A-minimal-example","page":"Getting Started","text":"Every downstream package — periodic lattices, quasicrystals, the Monte Carlo runtime — builds on top of AbstractLattice. LatticeCore itself ships two concrete reference implementations so you can exercise the full stack without any other dependency.\n\nusing LatticeCore\nusing StaticArrays\n\n# 1D periodic chain of 8 sites with default Ising site type\nchain = LineLattice(8, PeriodicAxis())\n\nnum_sites(chain)            # 8\nposition(chain, 1)          # SVector(1.0)\nneighbors(chain, 1)         # [8, 2]       (wraps to the far end)\nsite_type(chain, 1)         # IsingSite{Int8}()\n\n# 4x4 periodic square lattice\nsq = SimpleSquareLattice(4, 4, PeriodicAxis())\n\nnum_sites(sq)               # 16\nposition(sq, 5)             # SVector(1.0, 2.0)\nneighbors(sq, 5)             # [6, 4, 9, 1]\nis_bipartite(sq)            # true (both axis lengths are even)\n\n# Cylinder: periodic in x, open in y\ncyl = SimpleSquareLattice(4, 4,\n    LatticeBoundary((PeriodicAxis(), OpenAxis())))\nperiodicity(cyl)            # Aperiodic()  (one axis is open)\n\n# Reciprocal lattice and a naive structure factor\nml = reciprocal_lattice(sq)\nstate = Int8[1 for _ in 1:num_sites(sq)]           # ferromagnet\nstructure_factor(sq, state, SVector(0.0, 0.0))     # ≈ 16.0","title":"A minimal example"},{"category":"section","location":"getting_started/#The-concepts","page":"Getting Started","text":"A lattice in LatticeCore is described along five independent axes:\n\nTopology — which candidate bonds exist between sites. Captured by the topology trait and the lattice's native neighbour function.\nBoundary — how those candidate bonds wrap (or don't) at the edges of the lattice. Expressed as a LatticeBoundary composed of per-axis AbstractAxisBC and an optional AbstractBoundaryModifier.\nCoordinate system — how a physical position is identified: as a RealSpace point, a LatticeCoord (cell index + geometric sublattice), or a HigherDimCoord for cut-and-project quasicrystals.\nGeometric sublattice — the A/B/C role of a site within its unit cell. Lives in LatticeCoord.sublattice.\nSite type — the physical degree of freedom on the site (IsingSite, XYSite, HeisenbergSite, ...), stored via an AbstractSiteLayout (UniformLayout, SublatticeLayout, ExplicitLayout).\n\nThese are deliberately orthogonal: you can put any site type on any layout on any boundary on any topology, and they compose via multiple dispatch without changing core types.","title":"The concepts"},{"category":"section","location":"getting_started/#Guarding-Monte-Carlo-entry-points","page":"Getting Started","text":"Monte Carlo algorithms can only run on finite lattices. Guard the entry point of your run! function with require_finite:\n\nfunction run!(rng, state, lat::AbstractLattice, model, alg; kwargs...)\n    require_finite(lat)\n    # ... safe to iterate 1:num_sites(lat) from here ...\nend\n\nFor a conceptually-infinite structure, lower it to a finite lattice first with materialize:\n\nabstract_fibonacci = InfiniteFibonacci(...)          # downstream package\nlat = materialize(abstract_fibonacci; depth = 12)    # FiniteSize\nrun!(rng, state, lat, model, alg)","title":"Guarding Monte Carlo entry points"},{"category":"section","location":"getting_started/#Where-to-go-next","page":"Getting Started","text":"AbstractLattice interface\nBoundary conditions\nCoordinate systems\nSite types and layouts\nMomentum space\nLazy / infinite lattices","title":"Where to go next"},{"category":"section","location":"concepts/reciprocal_and_brillouin/#Reciprocal-lattice-and-Brillouin-zone","page":"Reciprocal lattice and Brillouin zone","text":"The real-space picture — points, bonds, sublattices — is half the story. The other half lives in momentum space: the reciprocal lattice, the Brillouin zone, and the structure factor. Everything LatticeCore exposes under reciprocal_lattice, monkhorst_pack, BraggPeakSet, and structure_factor ultimately comes from the content of this column.","title":"Reciprocal lattice and Brillouin zone"},{"category":"section","location":"concepts/reciprocal_and_brillouin/#The-reciprocal-lattice","page":"Reciprocal lattice and Brillouin zone","text":"Given a Bravais lattice with real-space basis mathbfa_1 dots mathbfa_d, the reciprocal basis mathbfb_1 dots mathbfb_d is defined by the orthogonality relation\n\nmathbfa_i cdot mathbfb_j = 2pi  delta_ij\n\nWriting A for the real-space basis matrix and B for the reciprocal one,\n\nB = 2pi  (A^-top)\n\nThe reciprocal lattice is then the set\n\nmathbfG = m_1 mathbfb_1 + dots + m_d mathbfb_d\nqquad m_i in mathbbZ\n\nReciprocal lattice vectors are distinguished by a single property: for every real-space lattice point mathbfR,\n\ne^-i mathbfG cdot mathbfR = 1\n\nIn other words, mathbfG and mathbfG + mathbfb_i produce identical plane waves sampled at the crystal points. Up to reciprocal lattice translations, only mathbfk values in a single primitive cell of reciprocal space are physically distinct. That primitive cell is the Brillouin zone.\n\nIn LatticeCore, reciprocal_basis(ml) returns the B matrix for a PeriodicMomentumLattice, and the orthogonality relation is one of the package's unit tests: a_i · b_j ≈ 2π δ_ij is checked directly against the reference square lattice.","title":"The reciprocal lattice"},{"category":"section","location":"concepts/reciprocal_and_brillouin/#The-Brillouin-zone","page":"Reciprocal lattice and Brillouin zone","text":"The first Brillouin zone is the Wigner–Seitz cell of the reciprocal lattice: the set of k-points that are closer to the origin Gamma than to any other reciprocal lattice vector. For the square lattice it is the square (-pi pi times (-pi pi; for the triangular lattice it is a hexagon; for the honeycomb, a hexagon rotated by 30°.\n\nThe Brillouin zone matters because every physical quantity that depends on momentum is periodic in reciprocal space. Band structures, structure factors, form factors — they all repeat with period mathbfG, so all the independent information fits inside a single BZ.\n\nLatticeCore's reference implementations do not enumerate Wigner–Seitz cells (that is topology-specific work for Lattice2D.jl). What they do provide is a uniform sampler of the BZ interior — the monkhorst_pack mesh.","title":"The Brillouin zone"},{"category":"section","location":"concepts/reciprocal_and_brillouin/#Sampling:-Monkhorst–Pack-and-Γ-centred-grids","page":"Reciprocal lattice and Brillouin zone","text":"Monte Carlo simulations on a finite L_1 times L_2 times dots times L_d lattice can only resolve a finite set of k-points, namely those compatible with the sample's periodicity:\n\nmathbfk in left fracn_iL_i mathbfb_i right_n_i = 0^L_i - 1\n\nLatticeCore ships two equivalent ways to enumerate these points:\n\ngamma_centered(basis, mesh) uses n_i  L_i, including the Gamma point exactly.\nmonkhorst_pack(basis, mesh) uses (n_i + 12)L_i - 12, which centres the mesh on Gamma but avoids it. This is the convention solid-state codes use for integrating smooth functions over the BZ.\n\nBoth return the same number of k-points, prod_i L_i, matching the number of sites in the corresponding real-space sample.","title":"Sampling: Monkhorst–Pack and Γ-centred grids"},{"category":"section","location":"concepts/reciprocal_and_brillouin/#The-structure-factor","page":"Reciprocal lattice and Brillouin zone","text":"Given a scalar field s_i defined on the lattice points, the structure factor is the modulus squared of its spatial Fourier transform:\n\nS(mathbfk) = frac1N\n    left sum_i = 1^N s_i  e^-i mathbfk cdot mathbfr_i right^2\n\nIt tells you, at a single configuration, how much Fourier weight sits at each momentum. Three sanity checks encode the physics:\n\nFerromagnet, s_i equiv 1. The sum is simply N, so S(0) = N, and — crucially — S(mathbfG) = N for every reciprocal lattice vector, because every such mathbfG is equivalent to Gamma. Away from mathbfG the sum over sites vanishes by destructive interference. This is verified in LatticeCore's test suite.\nNéel antiferromagnet on a bipartite lattice, s_i = (-1)^mathbfx + mathbfy on a square. The sum peaks at mathbfk = (pi pi), giving S(pi pi) = N and S(0) = 0. Bipartiteness is exactly the condition that this momentum lies on the reciprocal lattice.\nDisorder gives S(mathbfk) approx 1 on average — a useful null baseline when looking for order.\n\nThose checks are what LatticeCore's test_structure_factor.jl actually runs against structure_factor.","title":"The structure factor"},{"category":"section","location":"concepts/reciprocal_and_brillouin/#Implementation-notes","page":"Reciprocal lattice and Brillouin zone","text":"LatticeCore uses the naive O(N) per k-point formula above because it is transparent, correct on any lattice — including quasicrystals, which have no FFT-friendly mesh — and fast enough for the reference implementations. Production-grade codes on regular meshes can specialise structure_factor for FFT-based evaluation without changing the API.\n\nFor quasicrystals, mathbfk is no longer drawn from a discrete reciprocal lattice but from a dense Fourier module projected from a higher-dimensional periodic lattice. LatticeCore ships the type skeletons HyperReciprocalLattice and BraggPeakSet; the enumeration algorithm itself lives in QuasiCrystal.jl. See Quasiperiodic order for the column on this.","title":"Implementation notes"},{"category":"section","location":"concepts/reciprocal_and_brillouin/#Further-reading","page":"Reciprocal lattice and Brillouin zone","text":"Ashcroft–Mermin, Ch. 5 and Ch. 6 (reciprocal lattice and Bragg diffraction).\nMonkhorst & Pack, Phys. Rev. B 13 5188 (1976) — the original paper on special k-points.\nL. Landau and E. Lifshitz, Statistical Physics Part 1, § 116 (fluctuation–response and the structure factor).","title":"Further reading"},{"category":"section","location":"concepts/boundary_conditions/#Boundary-conditions","page":"Boundary conditions","text":"This column is still being written. In the meantime, the physics that drives LatticeCore's per-axis LatticeBoundary abstraction:\n\nPeriodic (PBC): the lattice is wrapped onto a torus. It is the gentlest finite-size approximation — every site looks like a bulk site — and it lets the reciprocal lattice remain discrete.\nOpen (OBC): the sample has real surfaces. Edge / corner sites have fewer neighbours, and the discrete reciprocal lattice no longer strictly applies.\nCylinder / strip: a mix of PBC and OBC along different axes. Ubiquitous in DMRG and tensor-network simulations because it tames edge effects while keeping a manageable transverse size.\nTwisted PBC: periodic with a phase factor e^itheta attached to bonds that cross the boundary. This is how a lattice model sees a magnetic flux, a twist angle, or a supercurrent. The topology is unchanged; only the phase accumulates.\nSine-square deformation (SSD): a non-topological modifier that multiplies bond energies by a smooth envelope sin^2(pi x  L). SSD is topologically open but reproduces periodic-like ground states surprisingly well, which is useful when you want PBC physics without the PBC integration pain.","title":"Boundary conditions"},{"category":"section","location":"concepts/boundary_conditions/#Why-per-axis?","page":"Boundary conditions","text":"Classical physics codes usually hardcode \"PBC\" or \"OBC\" as a global flag. That is wrong as soon as you want a cylinder — the axis choices stop being symmetric. LatticeCore follows the convention used by DMRG / tensor-network libraries and stores one AbstractAxisBC per axis, plus one AbstractBoundaryModifier, in a composite LatticeBoundary. Adding a new BC kind means adding a new AbstractAxisBC subtype and a method for apply_axis_bc; no core code changes.","title":"Why per-axis?"},{"category":"section","location":"concepts/boundary_conditions/#TODO","page":"Boundary conditions","text":"Diagram: PBC torus, OBC square, cylinder, Möbius.\nWorked example: dispersion of a 1D tight-binding chain under PeriodicAxis, OpenAxis, and TwistedAxis.\nFinite-size scaling and the role of BC in extracting bulk-limit quantities.\nWhere SSD fits in the modifier slot of LatticeBoundary.","title":"TODO"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/lazy_infinite/#Lazy-/-infinite-lattices","page":"Lazy / infinite lattices","text":"This guide is a work in progress. See the concept column Quasiperiodic order for the physical motivation.","title":"Lazy / infinite lattices"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/lazy_infinite/#Size-traits","page":"Lazy / infinite lattices","text":"LatticeCore describes the extent of a lattice through an AbstractSizeTrait value attached to every concrete lattice via size_trait:\n\nFiniteSize — ordinary finite lattice. MC is safe.\nInfiniteSize — conceptually infinite. MC is not safe; use the lattice only for spectral / analytic work.\nQuasiInfiniteSize — conceptually infinite but expected to be materialised at a cutoff before use.\n\nis_finite(lat) is a one-liner derived from this trait and is the predicate Monte Carlo algorithms should guard on.","title":"Size traits"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/lazy_infinite/#The-infinite-abstract-finite-materialisation-pattern","page":"Lazy / infinite lattices","text":"LatticeCore ships two small hooks for this pattern:\n\nmaterialize(abstract; kwargs...) — a generic function with no typed supertype. A package that defines an \"infinite abstract\" type (e.g. InfiniteFibonacci) adds a method for it that returns a FiniteSize concrete lattice.\nrequire_finite(lat) — an assertion guard that MC entry points should call:\n\nfunction run!(rng, state, lat::AbstractLattice, model, alg; kwargs...)\n    require_finite(lat)\n    # ... safe from here ...\nend\n\nThis pattern mirrors the momentum-space counterpart: HyperReciprocalLattice is the \"infinite abstract\" that becomes a finite BraggPeakSet at a cutoff.","title":"The infinite-abstract → finite-materialisation pattern"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/lazy_infinite/#TODO","page":"Lazy / infinite lattices","text":"Example: a custom InfiniteChain type + matching materialize that produces a LineLattice at a chosen depth.\nWhen real on-demand laziness (as opposed to materialisation) makes sense, and the open design questions around it.","title":"TODO"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/lazy_infinite/#See-also","page":"Lazy / infinite lattices","text":"Concepts: Quasiperiodic order\nReference: Lazy / infinite","title":"See also"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/reference_lattices/#Reference-lattices-API","page":"Reference lattices","text":"LatticeCore ships two minimal concrete lattices so the interface can be exercised end-to-end without any downstream package. These are reference implementations, not production lattices — the full 2D catalogue (triangular, honeycomb, kagome, ...) lives in Lattice2D.jl.","title":"Reference lattices API"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/reference_lattices/#LatticeCore.LineLattice","page":"Reference lattices","text":"LineLattice{T, B, L}(N, boundary, layout)\n\nA 1D linear chain of N sites with unit spacing.\n\nboundary is a LatticeBoundary whose single axis component is one of PeriodicAxis, OpenAxis, or TwistedAxis.\nlayout is an AbstractSiteLayout that decides which AbstractSiteType lives on each site. The default is UniformLayout(IsingSite()).\n\nLatticeCore's simplest reference implementation; paired with SimpleSquareLattice it is also the canonical mock used by downstream Monte Carlo unit tests.\n\nExamples\n\n# Default: PBC + UniformLayout(IsingSite())\nline = LineLattice(5)\n\n# Open boundary chain\nchain = LineLattice(5, OpenAxis())\n\n# Custom layout (e.g. XY sites)\nxy = LineLattice(5; layout = UniformLayout(XYSite()))\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LatticeCore.LineLattice"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/reference_lattices/#LatticeCore.SimpleSquareLattice","page":"Reference lattices","text":"SimpleSquareLattice{T, B <: LatticeBoundary}(Lx, Ly, boundary)\n\nA 2D square lattice of Lx × Ly sites with unit spacing. boundary is a LatticeBoundary whose two axis components independently select between PeriodicAxis, OpenAxis, and TwistedAxis — so mixed BCs (e.g. cylinders) are supported natively.\n\nLatticeCore's 2D reference implementation. It exists so that the core interface can be exercised end-to-end without depending on Lattice2D.jl. The production-grade 2D lattice, with the full topology catalogue (triangular, honeycomb, kagome, ...), sublattice site types, and reciprocal-lattice machinery, lives in Lattice2D.jl.\n\nSite indexing\n\nSites are laid out in row-major order:\n\nsite_index(x, y) = (y - 1) * Lx + x     # 1 <= x <= Lx, 1 <= y <= Ly\n\nso sites 1..Lx form the bottom row, Lx+1..2Lx the next, and so on.\n\nExamples\n\n# Default: 2D PBC\nsq = SimpleSquareLattice(3, 3)\n\n# Uniform open boundary\nopen_sq = SimpleSquareLattice(3, 3, OpenAxis())\n\n# Cylinder: periodic in x, open in y\ncylinder = SimpleSquareLattice(3, 3, LatticeBoundary((PeriodicAxis(), OpenAxis())))\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LatticeCore.SimpleSquareLattice"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/reference_lattices/#LatticeCore.basis_vectors","page":"Reference lattices","text":"basis_vectors(lat::LineLattice) → SMatrix{1, 1, T}\n\nReal-space basis matrix. For the unit-spacing reference chain this is the 1×1 identity.\n\n\n\n\n\nbasis_vectors(lat::SimpleSquareLattice) → SMatrix{2, 2, T}\n\nReal-space basis matrix. For the unit-spacing reference square this is the 2×2 identity.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LatticeCore.basis_vectors"},{"category":"section","location":"concepts/site_types_and_spins/#Site-types-and-spin-models","page":"Site types and spin models","text":"This column is still being written. The short version:","title":"Site types and spin models"},{"category":"section","location":"concepts/site_types_and_spins/#What-is-a-site-type?","page":"Site types and spin models","text":"A site type tells LatticeCore what mathematical object lives on a single lattice site and how to sample it uniformly at random. Concretely, AbstractSiteType requires\n\nstate_type — the Julia type that stores one state\nrandom_state — a uniform sampler\n\nand optionally\n\nzero_state, domain, and element_type for lifting the DOF onto bonds / plaquettes / cells.","title":"What is a site type?"},{"category":"section","location":"concepts/site_types_and_spins/#The-classical-spin-ladder","page":"Site types and spin models","text":"The archetypal site types form a ladder of increasing continuous symmetry:\n\nSite type State space Symmetry group Models\nIsingSite -1 +1 mathbbZ_2 Ising, RBIM\nPottsSite{Q} 1 dots Q S_Q Potts, clock\nXYSite circle S^1 U(1) = SO(2) XY, Villain\nHeisenbergSite sphere S^2 subset mathbbR^3 SO(3) Heisenberg\nEmptySite singleton trivial vacancies\n\nThe pattern is standard condensed-matter: as you move down the table, the Mermin–Wagner theorem kicks in (no long-range order in 2D for d geq 2 continuous groups at finite T), the energy landscape becomes smoother, and the numerically appropriate Monte Carlo update changes (single-flip → cluster → overrelaxation, cluster → worm).","title":"The classical spin ladder"},{"category":"section","location":"concepts/site_types_and_spins/#Why-this-lives-in-LatticeCore","page":"Site types and spin models","text":"A Monte Carlo runtime like Lattice2DMonteCarlo.jl needs to dispatch its local Hamiltonian on the site types at either end of a bond. Making AbstractSiteType part of LatticeCore — rather than the MC layer — means that every concrete lattice in the stack can describe what it carries, regardless of which MC runtime later consumes it.","title":"Why this lives in LatticeCore"},{"category":"section","location":"concepts/site_types_and_spins/#TODO","page":"Site types and spin models","text":"Worked example: how the Hamiltonian dispatch actually works when A and B sublattices carry different site types (SublatticeLayout).\nHeisenberg uniform sphere sampler (Marsaglia / Archimedes) and why HeisenbergSite uses SVector{3, Float64}.\nMermin–Wagner in one paragraph and how it motivates the Ising → Potts → XY → Heisenberg escalation.\nHow to build a custom site type for a bond-centered DOF (dimer model, gauge link) via element_type.","title":"TODO"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/lattice/#Lattice-interface","page":"Lattice interface","text":"This guide walks through the AbstractLattice interface — the minimum a concrete lattice must implement, the trait-based extension points, and the default methods that come for free.\n\nFor the physics and mathematical background, see the concept column Lattices and unit cells.","title":"Lattice interface"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/lattice/#The-abstract-type","page":"Lattice interface","text":"abstract type AbstractLattice{D, T} end\n\nThe two type parameters are:\n\nD::Int — the physical dimension (1, 2, 3, ...)\nT<:Real — the numeric type used to store positions (typically Float64)\n\nNothing else lives in the type parameters. Boundary conditions, topology, indexing, and site types are all stored as fields on the concrete subtype, and dispatched through multiple dispatch and trait objects.","title":"The abstract type"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/lattice/#Required-interface","page":"Lattice interface","text":"A concrete subtype must implement these:\n\nMethod Returns\nnum_sites Int\nposition SVector{D, T}\nneighbors AbstractVector{Int} (pinpoint)\nboundary LatticeBoundary\nsite_layout AbstractSiteLayout\nsize_trait AbstractSizeTrait\n\nnum_sites may be undefined — or may throw — when the lattice's size_trait is InfiniteSize.","title":"Required interface"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/lattice/#Free-defaults","page":"Lattice interface","text":"Once the required methods are in place, LatticeCore derives:\n\ndimension and Base.eltype from the type parameters\npositions and bonds as lazy iterators from position / neighbors\nneighbor_bonds from neighbors\nis_finite, is_bipartite, reciprocal_support, periodicity, topology as safe defaults that can be overridden\nsite_type(lat, i) by delegating to site_layout","title":"Free defaults"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/lattice/#Trait-based-extension","page":"Lattice interface","text":"Extension points use value types rather than flags, so dispatch stays compile-time. The main traits are:\n\nTopologyTrait — {:square}, {:honeycomb}, etc.\nPeriodic / Aperiodic — is the underlying crystal periodic?\nAbstractReciprocalSupport — does this lattice admit a Bravais reciprocal (HasReciprocal), a dense Fourier module (HasFourierModule), or neither (NoReciprocal)?\nAbstractSizeTrait — FiniteSize, InfiniteSize, or QuasiInfiniteSize.","title":"Trait-based extension"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/lattice/#API-reference","page":"Lattice interface","text":"Lattice\nBond\nTraits\nReference lattices","title":"API reference"},{"category":"section","location":"concepts/quasiperiodic_order/#Quasiperiodic-order","page":"Quasiperiodic order","text":"This column is still being written. The short version:","title":"Quasiperiodic order"},{"category":"section","location":"concepts/quasiperiodic_order/#Aperiodic-but-ordered","page":"Quasiperiodic order","text":"Most of condensed matter physics takes periodicity for granted: a Bravais lattice, translated forever. But there is a family of structures — Penrose tilings, Ammann–Beenker tilings, Fibonacci chains, icosahedral quasicrystals — that are not periodic in the usual sense yet are still highly ordered. They have long-range bond orientational order, crystallographically-forbidden point symmetries (5-fold for Penrose, 8-fold for Ammann–Beenker, 10-fold for decagonal quasicrystals), and sharp Bragg peaks in their diffraction patterns.","title":"Aperiodic but ordered"},{"category":"section","location":"concepts/quasiperiodic_order/#Cut-and-project","page":"Quasiperiodic order","text":"The cleanest way to generate these structures is cut-and-project:\n\nStart with a periodic lattice in a higher dimension mathbbZ^D_texthyper. For Penrose, D_texthyper = 5; for Ammann–Beenker, 4; for the Fibonacci chain, 2.\nChoose a physical subspace of dimension D_textphys (the plane or line you actually want to look at) and a perpendicular subspace of dimension D_texthyper - D_textphys (the \"internal\" space).\nPick an acceptance window in the perpendicular space: a bounded shape that acts as a stencil.\nA higher-dimensional lattice point lands in the physical structure if and only if its perpendicular projection sits inside the acceptance window.\n\nThe resulting set of physical-space points is the quasicrystal. It inherits the higher-dimensional translation symmetry but projects it irrationally, which is why the structure is dense in Fourier space yet discrete in physical space.\n\nLatticeCore captures the scaffolding with the types AcceptanceWindow, HyperReciprocalLattice, and BraggPeakSet; the concrete algorithms that build them live in QuasiCrystal.jl.","title":"Cut and project"},{"category":"section","location":"concepts/quasiperiodic_order/#Fourier-module-and-Bragg-peaks","page":"Quasiperiodic order","text":"Because a quasicrystal inherits its translation symmetry from a higher-dimensional periodic lattice, its Fourier transform is a finite set of Bragg peaks projected through the same matrix pi_parallel. Unlike a Bravais reciprocal lattice, the set of peak locations is dense in physical reciprocal space — but only finitely many peaks are bright, and their intensities are given by the Fourier transform of the acceptance window evaluated in the perpendicular space.\n\nA BraggPeakSet is what you get after fixing a cutoff: the finite list of peaks whose intensities exceed some threshold, stored together with their original higher-dimensional indices so you can walk back to the hyper lattice.\n\nCrucially, BraggPeakSet subtypes AbstractMomentumLattice, so the same structure_factor code paths that work on a PeriodicMomentumLattice work on a quasicrystal.","title":"Fourier module and Bragg peaks"},{"category":"section","location":"concepts/quasiperiodic_order/#TODO","page":"Quasiperiodic order","text":"Worked example: Fibonacci chain at depth = 8, a picture of its acceptance window, and the first handful of Bragg peaks.\nDiagram of cut-and-project in 2D projecting down to 1D.\nThe role of golden ratio φ in the Fibonacci case and how it shows up as the eigenvalue of the substitution matrix.\nWhen pseudo-Brillouin zones are a useful concept for quasicrystals and when they break down.","title":"TODO"},{"category":"section","location":"concepts/quasiperiodic_order/#Further-reading","page":"Quasiperiodic order","text":"M. Senechal, Quasicrystals and Geometry (Cambridge, 1995).\nM. Baake and U. Grimm, Aperiodic Order, Vol. 1 (Cambridge, 2013).\nZ. M. Stadnik (ed.), Physical Properties of Quasicrystals (Springer, 1999).","title":"Further reading"},{"category":"section","location":"#LatticeCore.jl","page":"Home","text":"LatticeCore is the abstract interface layer for a family of Julia packages that simulate physical systems on geometric lattices. It defines the types and trait vocabulary that every lattice — periodic or aperiodic, finite or conceptually infinite — must implement, and then ships a pair of minimal reference implementations (LineLattice, SimpleSquareLattice) plus a momentum-space layer so the interface is verifiable end-to-end without any other dependency.","title":"LatticeCore.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"#What-LatticeCore-is","page":"Home","text":"A single abstract type hierarchy rooted at AbstractLattice{D, T}, parameterised only on the physical dimension D and the numeric type T. Everything else — boundary conditions, topology, indexing, site types — is a field, composed via multiple dispatch and trait objects.\nA trait-based extension model (TopologyTrait, Periodic / Aperiodic, HasReciprocal / HasFourierModule / NoReciprocal, the size trait hierarchy) so new lattice kinds can plug in without touching core types.\nA per-axis boundary condition abstraction (LatticeBoundary) so cylinders (PBC × OBC) and flux-twisted lattices are as easy to describe as uniform PBC.\nA clean split between geometric sublattice (A/B on a honeycomb, A/B/C on kagome) and site type (the physical DOF living on the site: Ising spin, XY rotor, Heisenberg vector, vacancy).\nA momentum-space layer (AbstractMomentumLattice, PeriodicMomentumLattice, structure_factor) plus the type skeletons HyperReciprocalLattice / BraggPeakSet so quasicrystal Fourier analysis can ride the same observer codepaths as periodic lattices.\nA lazy-friendly interface: position(lat, i) and neighbors(lat, i) are pinpoint, positions(lat) and bonds(lat) are iterators. Infinite structures can be expressed abstractly and lowered to finite lattices through materialize.","title":"What LatticeCore is"},{"category":"section","location":"#What-LatticeCore-is-**not**","page":"Home","text":"A production-grade 2D lattice catalogue (triangular, honeycomb, kagome, Lieb, Shastry–Sutherland, ...). Those live in Lattice2D.jl and are built on top of LatticeCore.\nA quasicrystal generator. Cut-and-project Penrose / Ammann–Beenker / Fibonacci, their acceptance windows, and the Bragg-peak enumerator live in QuasiCrystal.jl.\nA Monte Carlo runtime. Classical MC models and algorithms live in Lattice2DMonteCarlo.jl; LatticeCore only supplies the site-type system and the require_finite guard they rely on.\nA visualisation layer. Plots / Makie integration lives in package extensions on top of the concrete-lattice packages, not here.","title":"What LatticeCore is not"},{"category":"section","location":"#Package-layout","page":"Home","text":"LatticeCore.jl            (this package, abstract interface)\n      │\n      ├── Lattice2D.jl        (periodic 2D catalogue)\n      │        │\n      ├── QuasiCrystal.jl     (cut-and-project quasicrystals)\n      │        │\n      └── ────┬─┘\n              ▼\n     Lattice2DMonteCarlo.jl   (classical MC runtime)","title":"Package layout"},{"category":"section","location":"#Design-principles","page":"Home","text":"One abstract type, minimal parameters. AbstractLattice{D, T}. BC / site type / indexing live in fields.\nNo isa branching. Every variant goes through multiple dispatch or a Holy trait.\nSeparation of concerns. Topology (which bonds exist), boundary (how they wrap), modifier (how they reweight), geometric sublattice, and physical site type are five independent axes.\nLazy-friendly I/F. Iterators everywhere; no method requires a full site enumeration.\nBreaking changes are cheap in the 0.x era. The whole stack is pre-1.0 and evolves with the architecture notes in the parent repo.","title":"Design principles"},{"category":"section","location":"#Further-reading","page":"Home","text":"Getting Started — install, construct a lattice, measure it.\nGuide — concept-by-concept walkthrough.\nAPI Reference — generated from docstrings.\nDesign Notes — summary of the key architectural decisions and pointers to the long-form notes.","title":"Further reading"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/coordinates/#Coordinate-systems-API","page":"Coordinates","text":"See Coordinate systems and indexing for the narrative version.","title":"Coordinate systems API"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/coordinates/#Abstract-type","page":"Coordinates","text":"","title":"Abstract type"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/coordinates/#Concrete-coordinate-kinds","page":"Coordinates","text":"","title":"Concrete coordinate kinds"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/coordinates/#Conversions","page":"Coordinates","text":"","title":"Conversions"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/coordinates/#LatticeCore.AbstractCoordinate","page":"Coordinates","text":"AbstractCoordinate{D}\n\nAbstract supertype for lattice coordinate representations. Concrete subtypes describe the same lattice point in different spaces:\n\nRealSpace — Cartesian coordinates\nLatticeCoord — unit cell index + geometric sublattice\nHigherDimCoord — higher-dimensional projection (for cut-and-project quasicrystals)\n\nD is the physical dimension (the dimension of the real space the lattice lives in), which is the same for every concrete subtype that describes a given lattice point.\n\nConversions between coordinate systems are performed by to_real, to_lattice, and to_hyper. These functions dispatch on the concrete lattice type because the conversion depends on the lattice basis and any higher-dimensional projection the lattice uses.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LatticeCore.AbstractCoordinate"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/coordinates/#LatticeCore.RealSpace","page":"Coordinates","text":"RealSpace{D, T}(x::SVector{D, T})\nRealSpace(x::NTuple{D, T})\n\nReal-space Cartesian coordinate in D dimensions with element type T.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LatticeCore.RealSpace"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/coordinates/#LatticeCore.LatticeCoord","page":"Coordinates","text":"LatticeCoord{D}(cell::NTuple{D, Int}, sublattice::Int = 1)\n\nLattice coordinate: per-axis unit cell index plus the (1-based) geometric sublattice id within the cell.\n\nThe sublattice field refers strictly to the geometric sublattice (the honeycomb A/B positions, the Kagome A/B/C, ...), not to the physical AbstractSiteType living on the site. Site types live on a separate axis and are designed in the site-type chapter of the architecture notes.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LatticeCore.LatticeCoord"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/coordinates/#LatticeCore.HigherDimCoord","page":"Coordinates","text":"HigherDimCoord{DPhys, DHyper, T}(hyper::SVector{DHyper, T})\n\nHigher-dimensional coordinate used by cut-and-project quasicrystals. DPhys is the physical dimension (the target of the projection) and DHyper is the host dimension (DHyper > DPhys). The lattice's projection matrix maps hyper back into DPhys-dimensional real space.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LatticeCore.HigherDimCoord"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/coordinates/#LatticeCore.to_real","page":"Coordinates","text":"to_real(lat::AbstractLattice, coord::AbstractCoordinate) → RealSpace\n\nConvert coord into a real-space Cartesian coordinate on lat. Concrete lattices should implement at least to_real(lat, ::LatticeCoord) (and, for quasicrystals, to_real(lat, ::HigherDimCoord)).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LatticeCore.to_real"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/coordinates/#LatticeCore.to_lattice","page":"Coordinates","text":"to_lattice(lat::AbstractLattice, coord::AbstractCoordinate) → LatticeCoord\n\nConvert coord into a lattice coordinate on lat. Concrete lattices should implement at least to_lattice(lat, ::RealSpace).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LatticeCore.to_lattice"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/coordinates/#LatticeCore.to_hyper","page":"Coordinates","text":"to_hyper(lat::AbstractLattice, coord::AbstractCoordinate) → HigherDimCoord\n\nConvert coord into a higher-dimensional hyper coordinate. Only meaningful for cut-and-project quasicrystals; other lattices may leave this unimplemented.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LatticeCore.to_hyper"}]
}
